-- certifications_people_delivery.sql
-- Why: the founder ruled that every position must hold and pass the top certifications for its
-- profession. This file authors the exam question banks for the five people/process/quality
-- certifications the org depends on (CSSBB, PMP, CPTD, SHRM-SCP, SHRM-CP): 40 questions each at
-- real exam difficulty, four options, one key, a two-sentence rationale, the syllabus area named
-- as ASQ / PMI / ATD / SHRM name it, ~25% marked 'hard' and the key letter spread evenly.
-- role_certification rows and the sitter are owned by a sibling file and are deliberately not
-- touched here. Every bank insert is guarded so the FIRST author of a shared bank stands.
--
-- Apply: node scripts/apply_sql.mjs scripts/sql/certifications_people_delivery.sql --apply --ddl

-- ============================================================================
-- CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt) — ASQ body of knowledge
-- ============================================================================
insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select * from (values
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Measure','exam','A stable filling process reports Cp = 1.62 and Cpk = 0.91. What does the Black Belt conclude and act on first?','The process is inherently incapable and variation must be reduced before anything else','The spread fits the specification comfortably but the mean is off centre, so centre the process first','The measurement system is inadequate and must be re-studied before any capability claim','The specification limits are too wide and should be tightened with the customer','B','Cp uses only the spread while Cpk also penalises the distance of the mean from the nearest limit, so a high Cp with a much lower Cpk is the signature of an off-centre process. Centring is normally cheaper and faster than variance reduction, so it is the first move.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Control','exam','On an X-bar and R chart the R chart shows one point above its upper control limit while the X-bar chart looks stable. What is the correct response?','Recompute the control limits using the most recent twenty subgroups','Treat both charts as stable because the X-bar chart shows no signal','Widen the R chart limits to three and a half sigma to reduce false alarms','Investigate and remove the special cause of variation on the R chart before interpreting the X-bar chart','D','The X-bar limits are computed from R-bar, so an out-of-control range chart makes the X-bar limits meaningless. Within-subgroup variation must be brought into control first.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Analyze','hard','A team must decide whether four suppliers differ in mean lead time. Sample sizes are unequal, residuals are approximately normal, but the Levene test rejects equal variances at 0.05. Which analysis is most defensible?','A Welch ANOVA, which does not assume equal variances across the four groups','A classical one-way ANOVA, because ANOVA is robust to any variance difference','Four pairwise t-tests at 0.05 each, reporting only the significant ones','A chi-square test of independence on lead time and supplier','A','Welch ANOVA relaxes the homogeneity-of-variance assumption that Levene has just rejected, so it protects the stated alpha. Classical ANOVA is only mildly robust and unequal sample sizes make the distortion worse, while repeated uncorrected t-tests inflate the family-wise error rate.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Define','exam','A sponsor asks for a project charter whose problem statement reads: quality is poor and customers are unhappy. What is the strongest objection a Black Belt should raise?','It names no owner, so no one can be held accountable for the result','It is too long and should be reduced to a single sentence for the steering committee','It contains no measurable baseline, gap or timeframe, so project success can never be judged','It mentions customers, which belongs in the voice-of-the-customer section instead','C','A problem statement must state what is wrong, how big it is against a measured baseline, and by when it must change, or the team cannot recognise completion. Ownership and section placement are secondary to measurability.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Measure','exam','A gauge R and R study on a critical dimension yields 8 percent study variation from repeatability and 27 percent from reproducibility. What is the priority corrective action?','Buy a higher resolution gauge, since the instrument is the dominant error source','Increase the number of parts in the study until the percentages fall','Standardise and retrain the operator method, because appraiser differences dominate the error','Accept the system, because the total is below 40 percent of study variation','C','Reproducibility is the appraiser-to-appraiser component, so a large reproducibility term points at inconsistent operator technique rather than the instrument. Standard work and training address it directly, while a new gauge would attack the smaller repeatability term.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Improve','exam','A team has seven suspected factors and a limited budget for runs. What design should it start with?','A resolution IV fractional factorial screening design to identify the vital few factors','A full factorial in all seven factors to obtain every interaction cleanly','A one-factor-at-a-time sequence, changing one input per run','A central composite response surface design in all seven factors','A','Screening designs are built exactly for the case of many candidate factors and few runs, and resolution IV keeps main effects clear of two-factor interactions. Full factorial and response surface designs in seven factors are far too expensive at this stage, and one-factor-at-a-time cannot detect interactions.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Control','hard','A p chart of daily defective rate has subgroup sizes that vary between 180 and 950 units. The team wants one fixed control limit line for the shop floor display. What is the correct guidance?','Use the average subgroup size to fix the limits, since the variation in n is small','Convert the chart to an individuals chart of the daily percentage instead','Plot the raw defective count on a c chart, which needs no sample size','Use varying limits, or a standardised Z chart, because the limits depend on the subgroup size','D','The p chart limit width is proportional to the square root of p-bar times one minus p-bar divided by n, so a fivefold change in n changes the limits materially. A standardised Z chart gives the single fixed line the floor wants without falsifying the signal.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Analyze','exam','A regression of cycle time on five predictors gives R-squared of 0.86 but the residual plot against fitted values shows a clear funnel shape. What should the Black Belt conclude?','The model is good because R-squared is high and the predictors are significant','The constant variance assumption is violated, so the standard errors and p-values are not trustworthy','The residuals are autocorrelated and a time-series model is required','Multicollinearity is present and one predictor must be dropped','B','A funnel-shaped residual plot is the classic picture of heteroscedasticity, which biases the standard errors even when the fit looks strong. A variance-stabilising transformation or weighted least squares is the usual remedy.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Measure','exam','A process produces 3 defects per unit opportunity set of 12 opportunities per unit across 500 units, with 210 defects found. What metric best supports comparison with a very different process?','Total defect count, because it is the number the plant manager recognises','Defects per million opportunities, because it normalises for unit complexity','First pass yield, because it counts units rather than defects','Percentage defective, because it is bounded between zero and one','B','DPMO divides defects by units times opportunities per unit, so processes of different complexity can be compared on one scale. Counts, yields and percent defective all embed the complexity of the unit and so cannot be compared across processes.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Organization-wide Planning and Deployment','exam','A newly appointed executive sponsor asks what her role in a Six Sigma deployment actually is. What is the most accurate answer?','To chair the daily team meetings and approve each statistical tool selection','To perform the measurement system analysis so the team can trust its data','To audit the control plan after closure and sign the financial benefit','To remove organisational barriers, secure resources and hold the tollgate reviews','D','The sponsor owns resources, priority and the removal of cross-functional obstacles, and confirms progress at tollgates. Tool selection and analysis belong to the Black Belt, and benefit validation belongs to finance.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Analyze','hard','Two proportions of escaped defects are compared before and after a change, with n of 40 and 3 events in one group. Which test is most appropriate?','The Fisher exact test, because the expected cell counts are too small for the chi-square approximation','A two-sample chi-square test with Yates correction, which is valid at any count','A two-sample t-test on the individual zero or one outcomes','A paired t-test, because the same process was measured twice','A','The chi-square approximation requires expected counts of roughly five or more, which three events in forty trials cannot supply. Fisher exact computes the null distribution directly and is valid at small counts.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Control','exam','A team closed a project six months ago and the improvement has decayed back toward baseline. Which control-plan failure most likely explains it?','The financial benefit was overstated by finance at closure','The team used an individuals chart instead of an X-bar chart','No process owner was assigned with a documented reaction plan for out-of-control signals','The improvement was verified with a hypothesis test rather than a designed experiment','C','Control plans hold gains only when a named owner monitors the metric and follows a defined reaction when it drifts. Chart type and benefit accounting do not cause decay by themselves.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Measure','exam','A destructive test prevents any part from being measured twice. What measurement system study is appropriate?','A standard crossed gauge R and R with three appraisers and ten parts','A bias study against a certified master alone, since repeatability cannot exist','A nested gauge R and R using homogeneous batches, with parts nested within operator','No study is possible, so the measurement error must be assumed negligible','C','When a part cannot be re-measured, repeatability can only be estimated across supposedly identical parts from the same batch, which is exactly the nested design. Assuming negligible error is never acceptable for a critical characteristic.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Define','exam','During project scoping the team finds the true bottleneck lies in a department outside the charter boundary. What is the correct action?','Escalate to the sponsor to rescope or re-charter the project before proceeding','Continue as chartered and note the bottleneck in the final report','Silently widen the scope, since the data justify it','Cancel the project because the charter is invalid','A','Scope changes belong to the sponsor because they change resources, stakeholders and expected benefit. Working outside the charter without sanction destroys the tollgate discipline that makes deployments credible.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Improve','hard','A two-level factorial shows factor A insignificant, factor B significant and the AB interaction strongly significant. What is the correct interpretation?','Drop A from the model because its main effect p-value exceeds 0.05','Report B alone as the driver and set A at its cheapest level','Re-run the experiment because a significant interaction with an insignificant main effect is impossible','Keep A in the model, because the effect of B depends on the level of A','D','Model hierarchy requires that a factor appearing in a significant interaction is retained even when its main effect is small. Setting A by cost alone would silently choose one of the two very different B responses.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Team Management','exam','Two team members repeatedly argue about the root cause and the meetings have stopped producing decisions. What should the Black Belt do first?','Escalate to the sponsor and request that one member be replaced','Redirect the disagreement to data by defining the experiment or analysis that would settle it','Take the decision personally to keep the schedule','Move to majority vote on the root cause at the next meeting','B','A Black Belt resolves technical conflict by converting opinion into a testable question, which restores progress without damaging the relationship. Escalation, authority and voting all decide by position rather than by evidence.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Measure','exam','Which sampling approach best protects a capability study from being contaminated by shift-to-shift differences?','Take one large convenience sample from whichever shift is available','Use rational subgroups drawn within shift, so between-shift variation appears between subgroups','Randomise units across all shifts inside every subgroup to average the effect out','Sample only the best-performing shift so the capability is not understated','B','Rational subgrouping deliberately keeps suspected sources of variation between subgroups rather than inside them, so the chart can detect them. Mixing shifts inside a subgroup inflates within-subgroup variation and hides the very effect of interest.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Analyze','exam','A team wants to know whether an ordinal customer satisfaction score differs across three service centres and the distributions are clearly skewed. Which test fits?','One-way ANOVA on the raw scores','A chi-square goodness-of-fit test','A paired t-test on the mean scores','The Kruskal-Wallis test, a nonparametric comparison of three or more independent groups','D','Kruskal-Wallis compares distributions across independent groups without a normality assumption and suits ordinal data. ANOVA assumes approximate normality of residuals and the other options do not answer the question asked.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Control','hard','An individuals and moving range chart of a slow, expensive process shows eight consecutive points above the centre line but none outside the limits. What should the team conclude?','A sustained shift in the mean has occurred and the cause should be investigated','The process is in control, because no point breached a control limit','The moving range chart must be recalculated before any conclusion','The limits should be recomputed to include the recent points','A','A run of eight points on one side of the centre line is a standard out-of-control rule, because such a run is very unlikely under a stable process. Recomputing limits to absorb the shift would erase the signal rather than explain it.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Organizational Process Management and Measures','exam','Which metric set best balances a process scorecard for a service operation?','Only lagging financial results, because they are what the board reads','Only leading process indicators, because they are actionable in the moment','A mix of leading process indicators and lagging outcome measures with defined owners','Only customer satisfaction, because it summarises everything else','C','Leading indicators allow intervention while lagging outcomes confirm the effect actually reached the customer or the ledger. A single class of metric either arrives too late to act on or is never validated against results.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Define','exam','A voice-of-the-customer study produces the statement: I want my quotation quickly. What is the correct next step for the team?','Record it verbatim in the charter as the project goal','Ask the customer to restate the requirement in statistical terms','Translate it into a critical-to-quality characteristic with a measure, target and specification limit','Assign it to the marketing function, since it concerns customer perception','C','A customer need is not usable until it becomes a measurable CTQ with a target and limits that the process can be judged against. Verbatim capture is the input to that translation, not a substitute for it.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Measure','exam','A distribution of transaction times is strongly right skewed and the team must estimate the proportion beyond a 48 hour limit. What is the safest approach?','Fit an appropriate non-normal distribution or transform the data before estimating the tail','Use the normal capability calculation because the sample size exceeds thirty','Report the sample percentage only and refuse any model-based estimate','Remove the long tail as outliers and then apply the normal calculation','A','Normal-based capability formulas grossly misestimate tail probabilities for skewed data, which is precisely where the specification sits. Fitting a suitable distribution or transforming the response keeps the tail estimate honest, whereas deleting the tail deletes the problem.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Analyze','hard','A team observes a strong positive correlation between the number of agents on shift and the number of complaints, and proposes cutting staff. What is the flaw?','The correlation coefficient was probably computed incorrectly','A correlation of that size is never statistically significant in service data','Complaints should have been modelled as a Poisson count rather than a continuous response','Volume is a lurking variable driving both staffing and complaints, so the correlation is not causal','D','Both variables rise with workload, so the association is confounded rather than causal and the proposed action would make service worse. Establishing causation needs the confounder controlled, by stratification, covariate adjustment or an experiment.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Improve','exam','Which criterion should decide between two improvement alternatives that both meet the CTQ target?','Whichever alternative the process owner proposed, to secure adoption','The alternative with the lower total cost of poor quality and the lower risk of failure modes','The alternative that uses the more advanced statistical technique','The alternative that can be implemented without any control plan','B','Improve-phase selection is an economic and risk decision once both options meet the requirement, so cost of poor quality and FMEA risk decide. Sophistication and political convenience are not selection criteria, and no option should ship without a control plan.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Control','exam','What is the primary purpose of a control plan handed to the process owner at closure?','To document the statistical software settings used during the project','To define what is measured, how often, by whom, and what action follows an out-of-control signal','To record the financial benefit for the finance function','To list the team members and their training levels','B','The control plan is the operational contract that keeps the improvement alive after the team disbands. Software settings, benefits and rosters belong in the project file, not in the plan the operator follows.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Measure','exam','An attribute agreement analysis shows appraisers agree with each other 92 percent of the time but agree with the known standard only 61 percent of the time. What does this indicate?','The measurement system is acceptable because appraiser agreement is high','More appraisers must be added to raise the agreement with the standard','The parts chosen were too easy and should be replaced with borderline parts','The appraisers share a consistent but incorrect criterion, so the operational definition must be corrected','D','High agreement among appraisers with low agreement to the standard means everyone is applying the same wrong rule. Fixing the operational definition and retraining against the standard is the remedy, not adding more appraisers.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Analyze','hard','A failure mode has severity 9, occurrence 2 and detection 3, giving an RPN of 54, while another has severity 3, occurrence 7 and detection 6, giving 126. How should the team prioritise?','Address the severity 9 mode first despite its lower RPN, because severity ranks safety and customer impact','Address the RPN 126 mode first, since RPN is the agreed priority number','Address neither, because both RPNs fall below the common threshold of 150','Recompute both using the criticality index before deciding anything','A','RPN is a crude product that can rank a high-severity failure below a nuisance, so modern FMEA practice treats high severity as a priority in its own right. Severity 9 implies a safety or major customer consequence that low occurrence does not make acceptable.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Team Management','exam','A cross-functional team is in the storming stage and progress has stalled. What intervention fits the stage?','Disband and reform the team with members who agree','Wait, because storming resolves itself without intervention','Clarify roles, decision rights and ground rules, and refocus the team on the charter','Move immediately to detailed task assignment and daily deadlines','C','Storming is a conflict over roles and influence, so the leader resolves it by making roles, decision rights and norms explicit. Tightening deadlines without settling roles usually deepens the conflict.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Organization-wide Planning and Deployment','exam','Finance disputes the claimed savings of a closed project. What is the correct Black Belt position?','Defend the team calculation, since the team measured the process directly','Withdraw the claim entirely to protect the credibility of the programme','Accept a finance-validated benefit figure, because programme credibility depends on an independent ledger','Split the difference between the two figures','C','A deployment survives only if benefits are validated by the function that owns the ledger, which removes the incentive to over-claim. Defending an unvalidated number risks the credibility of every other project.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Measure','exam','What does a sigma level of 4.5 with the customary 1.5 sigma shift correspond to in long-term defect terms?','Approximately 1350 defects per million opportunities','Approximately 3.4 defects per million opportunities','Approximately 233 defects per million opportunities','Approximately 66807 defects per million opportunities','A','A long-term 4.5 sigma capability corresponds to roughly 1350 DPMO, whereas the well-known 3.4 DPMO figure belongs to six sigma with the same 1.5 shift. Confusing the two overstates capability by more than two orders of magnitude.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Control','hard','A team wants to detect a small sustained mean shift of about one sigma as early as possible on an expensive continuous process. Which chart is best suited?','A standard Shewhart individuals chart, which is optimal for all shift sizes','A p chart of the proportion beyond specification','A pre-control chart using the specification limits','A CUSUM or EWMA chart, which accumulates evidence across successive points','D','Shewhart charts react to each point alone and are slow to detect shifts under about one and a half sigma, while CUSUM and EWMA accumulate small departures and signal much sooner. Pre-control uses specifications rather than process behaviour and is weaker still.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Define','exam','Which of these is the best expression of project scope in a charter?','A list of the statistical tools the team intends to use','The process start and end points, the units in scope and what is explicitly out of scope','The names of the executives who requested the project','The budget approved for the improvement','B','Scope is defined by process boundaries and inclusions and exclusions, so anyone can tell whether a given problem belongs to the project. Tools, sponsors and budget are charter content but do not delimit the work.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Improve','exam','A pilot of the improved process runs for two weeks and shows the target met. What must happen before full rollout?','Nothing, because the pilot met the target','Confirm the pilot covered the normal range of operating conditions and update the FMEA and control plan','Repeat the pilot until statistical significance reaches 0.001','Transfer ownership to finance for benefit tracking','B','A pilot only supports rollout if it exercised the conditions the full process will meet, and the risk assessment and control plan must be updated to the new design. Chasing extreme significance levels adds nothing once the practical target is demonstrated.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Analyze','exam','A multiple regression reports a variance inflation factor of 14 for one predictor. What is the practical consequence?','The model has no predictive value and should be discarded','The residuals are non-normal and must be transformed','The response variable has an outlier that must be removed','That coefficient is unstable and its sign or size cannot be interpreted reliably','D','A high VIF signals that the predictor is nearly a linear combination of the others, which inflates the variance of its estimated coefficient. Prediction may still be adequate, but individual coefficient interpretation is not.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Design for Six Sigma (DFSS) Frameworks and Methodologies','hard','A team is designing a new service rather than improving an existing one, and no baseline process exists. Which framework applies and why?','DMADV, because the design phases replace the analysis of an existing process that cannot be measured','DMAIC, because measure and analyse can always be applied to a proposed design','DMAIC with the improve phase removed, since there is nothing to improve','Neither, because Six Sigma applies only to manufacturing','A','DMAIC assumes an existing process with a measurable baseline and a gap to close, which a new design does not have. DMADV substitutes design and verify phases that translate customer requirements into a validated design.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Organizational Process Management and Measures','exam','A SIPOC diagram is being built for a quotation process. Which statement is correct?','It should contain the detailed decision logic of each step','It replaces the process map used later in the measure phase','It bounds the process at a high level by naming suppliers, inputs, process steps, outputs and customers','It should be built only after the measurement system analysis is complete','C','SIPOC is a scoping tool that sets the boundaries and stakeholders of the process before detail is added. Detailed logic belongs to the later process map, and SIPOC precedes measurement work rather than following it.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Team Management','exam','A Black Belt notices that the process operators were not consulted during solution design and are now resisting the rollout. What is the underlying failure?','The statistical analysis was not communicated in enough detail','The sponsor did not attend enough team meetings','The team omitted the people who own the daily work from the change, so there is no ownership','The control plan was written too early in the project','C','Solutions designed without the people who must run them predictably meet resistance, because those people have neither ownership nor a voice in feasibility. Involvement during design, not explanation afterwards, is what prevents it.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Measure','exam','A team needs the sample size for estimating a mean within plus or minus 2 units at 95 percent confidence with an assumed standard deviation of 8. Which relationship governs the answer?','n grows with the square of the ratio of standard deviation to the desired margin of error','n grows linearly with the standard deviation and linearly with the margin','n depends only on the population size and not on the standard deviation','n is fixed at thirty for any normal population','A','The half-width of a confidence interval is proportional to the standard deviation divided by the square root of n, so halving the margin quadruples the sample size. Population size does not enter for large populations and the rule of thirty concerns approximate normality, not precision.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Control','hard','After a genuine, verified improvement the control chart limits are still the pre-improvement limits, and every point now sits below the lower control limit. What should the team do?','Leave the limits alone so the improvement stays visible to management','Treat every point as a special cause and open an investigation for each','Switch to a specification-based chart to avoid the false alarms','Recompute the control limits from post-improvement data, once the new process is shown to be stable','D','Control limits describe the current process, so a verified shift makes the old limits obsolete and generates continual false signals. The correct practice is to re-baseline the limits after confirming stability of the improved process.'),
('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)','Design for Six Sigma (DFSS) Frameworks and Methodologies','exam','In a quality function deployment house of quality, what does the roof matrix capture?','The relative importance weights the customer places on each need','The correlations, positive or negative, between the technical requirements themselves','The competitive benchmark scores against rival offerings','The mapping of customer needs to technical requirements','B','The roof of the house records how technical requirements reinforce or conflict with one another, which is what exposes design trade-offs. Customer weights, benchmarks and the needs-to-requirements mapping occupy other parts of the house.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)');

-- ============================================================================
-- PMP (Project Management Professional) — PMI People / Process / Business Environment
-- ============================================================================
insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select * from (values
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','People','exam','A newly formed agile team misses its first two sprint commitments and morale is falling. What should the project manager do first?','Reassign the lowest performing team members to other work','Facilitate a retrospective so the team identifies its own impediments and adjusts its commitment','Increase the sprint length so more work fits inside the timebox','Report the team to the sponsor as underperforming','B','The retrospective is the built-in mechanism for a team to inspect and adapt its own way of working, and it preserves ownership. Reassignment, timebox changes and escalation act on symptoms without the team learning anything.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Process','exam','Halfway through a predictive project the customer requests a significant new feature. What is the correct first action?','Accept it if the team believes it can absorb the work','Refuse it because the scope baseline is approved','Add it to the next release without changing the current plan','Log a change request and take it through the integrated change control process for impact analysis','D','Integrated change control exists so the impact on scope, schedule, cost, risk and quality is assessed and decided by the authorised body. Accepting or refusing at the project manager level bypasses the governance the baseline depends on.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Business Environment','hard','A regulator publishes a new rule that will make the projects planned solution non-compliant six months after go-live. The sponsor wants to proceed unchanged to hit the date. What should the project manager do?','Document the compliance exposure, quantify it as a risk with its response options, and escalate for a governance decision','Proceed as directed, because the sponsor holds the decision authority','Halt the project immediately until legal counsel provides written clearance','Change the solution design unilaterally to meet the new rule','A','A project manager owes the organisation a documented, quantified statement of regulatory exposure and its options, and the decision then belongs to governance rather than to a single sponsor. Silent compliance and unilateral redesign both remove the decision from the people accountable for it.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Process','exam','A project has an earned value of 220000, a planned value of 260000 and an actual cost of 240000. What is the correct read?','The project is ahead of schedule and under budget','The project is on schedule but over budget','The project is behind schedule and over budget','The data are insufficient to judge either dimension','C','Earned value below planned value gives a negative schedule variance, and earned value below actual cost gives a negative cost variance. Both indices are under one, so the project is late and overspent.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','People','exam','Two senior team members disagree publicly over a technical approach and the disagreement is spilling into the wider team. Which conflict-resolution approach does PMI treat as generally best?','Withdraw and let time settle the disagreement','Compromise so that each side concedes half its position','Collaborate and problem-solve to reach a solution both can genuinely support','Use position power to impose a decision quickly','C','Collaboration or problem solving addresses the underlying interests and produces a durable decision the team owns. Withdrawal defers the problem, compromise leaves both sides partly dissatisfied, and forcing damages trust.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Business Environment','exam','A benefits management plan is being written before project execution. What must it contain?','The target benefits, how and when they will be measured, and who owns their realisation','The detailed work breakdown structure for the delivery phase','The risk register with quantified contingency reserve','The procurement strategy for external suppliers','A','A benefits management plan states what value is expected, the metrics and timing that will prove it, and the accountable owner after the project closes. Work breakdown, risk and procurement are project artefacts rather than benefit artefacts.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Process','hard','A predictive schedule has a critical path of 40 days and the sponsor needs 34. Two options exist: run design and build partly in parallel, or add contractors to the build. What distinguishes them?','Both are crashing techniques and cost the same in practice','Adding contractors is fast tracking and always increases cost; parallelism is crashing and does not','Fast tracking is the safe option and crashing should be avoided entirely','Overlapping the phases is fast tracking, which adds rework risk, while adding resources is crashing, which adds cost','D','Fast tracking overlaps activities that were sequential and buys time at the price of rework risk, while crashing adds resources and buys time at the price of cost. Naming the technique correctly is what lets the sponsor choose between risk and money.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','People','exam','A project manager is assigned to a functional organisation with limited authority and needs a scarce specialist held by another manager. What is the most effective approach?','Direct the specialist to join the project, citing project priority','Negotiate with the functional manager, using the sponsor and the business case as leverage','Hire an external contractor without consulting the functional manager','Reduce the scope so the specialist is not needed','B','In a functional structure the project manager has influence rather than authority, so resource acquisition is a negotiation supported by sponsorship and business justification. Direct instruction exceeds the authority actually held.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Process','exam','A team is using a hybrid approach where requirements are stable for infrastructure but volatile for the user interface. What is the most appropriate life cycle choice?','Force a single life cycle across the whole project for consistency of reporting','Run the infrastructure predictively and the interface iteratively, integrating at defined points','Run everything iteratively so nothing is locked down too early','Run everything predictively and manage interface change through change control','B','Life cycle selection follows the volatility of the requirements in each part of the work, which is exactly what a hybrid approach permits. Forcing one life cycle either freezes work that must evolve or leaves stable work without an efficient plan.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Business Environment','exam','A project delivers on time and on budget, but the expected business benefit does not materialise. What does this most likely reveal?','A failure of schedule and cost control during execution','A failure in quality assurance during testing','A failure of the procurement strategy','A failure to validate that the delivered output actually addressed the business need','D','Meeting the triple constraint proves the output was produced, not that it created value, which is the concern of the business case and benefits realisation. Weak requirements validation or an unsound business case is the usual cause.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Process','hard','A quantitative risk analysis yields a Monte Carlo P80 completion of 22 August while the deterministic schedule shows 5 August. How should the project manager use this?','Set the committed date near the P80 and hold the difference as schedule reserve owned by governance','Report the deterministic date, since it is the schedule the team built','Report the P80 as the new deterministic schedule and rebaseline every activity','Discard the simulation because it contradicts the approved schedule','A','The deterministic date reflects a single optimistic path while the P80 reflects the confidence level the organisation is willing to commit to, and the gap is the schedule reserve. Reserve is held at the project or programme level and released against realised risk, not spread silently into activity estimates.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','People','exam','A key stakeholder attends no meetings and does not respond to status reports, but blocks decisions late. What should the project manager do?','Remove the stakeholder from the distribution list to reduce noise','Escalate immediately to the sponsor for removal of the stakeholder','Reassess the stakeholder engagement strategy and agree a communication channel and cadence that this stakeholder will actually use','Continue current reporting and document the lack of response','C','Stakeholder engagement is adapted to the stakeholder rather than to the project managers convenience, so the channel, format and cadence must change. Removal and passive documentation leave the blocking behaviour untouched.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Process','exam','Which statement about the risk register and the issue log is correct?','They are the same artefact under two names','An issue is a risk whose probability is below ten percent','A risk is an uncertain future event, while an issue is a condition that has already occurred and requires action now','Risks are owned by the sponsor and issues by the team','C','A risk carries uncertainty and is managed by probability and impact responses, while an issue has materialised and needs resolution and an owner. Conflating the two removes the ability to act before impact.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','People','exam','A servant leader in an agile context is best described as someone who does what?','Removes impediments, builds the environment and lets the team decide how the work is done','Assigns tasks daily and tracks individual utilisation','Approves each technical decision before implementation','Represents only the customer interest inside the team','A','Servant leadership shifts the leaders role from directing work to enabling a self-organising team and clearing what blocks it. Task assignment and technical approval reintroduce the command structure that self-organisation replaces.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Business Environment','hard','An organisation runs a portfolio in which two projects each independently show a positive net present value but together exceed the available delivery capacity. What is the correct portfolio decision basis?','Deliver both, because both have a positive net present value','Deliver the cheaper one, because it consumes less capacity','Deliver the one with the earlier start date, to preserve commitments already made','Rank them by strategic contribution against the constrained resource and sequence accordingly','D','Portfolio selection under a binding constraint is a ranking problem against that constraint, not an independent accept-or-reject on each business case. Cost and start date are inputs to the ranking rather than the ranking itself.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Process','exam','A project uses a fixed-price contract and the seller discovers the work is far larger than bid. Who carries the cost risk?','The buyer, because the requirements were supplied by the buyer','The seller, because the price is fixed regardless of the sellers actual cost','It is shared equally by contract law','The risk transfers to the buyer once fifty percent of the work is complete','B','A firm fixed-price contract places cost risk on the seller, which is precisely why buyers pay a risk premium in the price. Cost risk only returns to the buyer where the buyer changes the scope through formal change control.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','People','exam','Team members in three time zones report that they never hear decisions until after they are made. What is the most effective corrective action?','Increase the frequency of the written status report','Publish decisions asynchronously in a shared, timestamped log and rotate any synchronous meeting time','Require every member to attend the existing meeting regardless of local time','Delegate all decisions to the members in the majority time zone','B','Distributed teams need decisions recorded asynchronously so no member depends on being awake, and rotating meeting times distributes the inconvenience fairly. More reports and forced attendance keep the same structural disadvantage.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Process','exam','A definition of done is being agreed for an agile team. What belongs in it?','The list of user stories planned for the release','The acceptance criteria specific to one particular story','The order in which stories will be pulled from the backlog','The conditions every increment must satisfy before it can be called complete, such as tested, reviewed and documented','D','The definition of done is a team-wide quality contract applying to every increment, while acceptance criteria are specific to a single story. Backlog content and ordering are separate concerns entirely.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Business Environment','hard','A project has consumed 70 percent of its budget and the business case now shows a negative net present value for the remaining work. What should the project manager recommend?','Recommend terminating or re-scoping the project, because sunk cost does not justify future spend','Continue, because 70 percent of the budget is already committed','Continue but cut quality to preserve the remaining budget','Transfer the project to another sponsor who may value it more','A','Money already spent is sunk and irrelevant to the forward decision, which turns only on whether the remaining spend earns a return. Continuing on the strength of past expenditure is the sunk-cost fallacy, and cutting quality destroys the benefit that justified the project.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Process','exam','A predictive project needs its scope baseline. Which set of documents forms it?','The charter, the business case and the benefits plan','The schedule, the cost estimate and the risk register','The approved scope statement, the work breakdown structure and the WBS dictionary','The requirements documentation and the stakeholder register','C','The scope baseline is precisely the approved scope statement together with the WBS and its dictionary, against which scope change is measured. The other documents are inputs to it or separate baselines.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','People','exam','What is the most reliable indicator that a team has genuinely become self-organising?','The project manager attends every daily standup','The team selects how to accomplish the work it has committed to and resolves most impediments itself','The team reports individual utilisation above ninety percent','The team never raises impediments to the project manager','C','Self-organisation shows in the team choosing its own method and clearing its own obstacles, while still escalating what it genuinely cannot resolve. A team that never escalates is usually hiding problems rather than solving them.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Process','exam','Which measure best tells an agile team whether it can meet a release forecast?','The empirical velocity across recent sprints applied to the remaining backlog','The number of story points committed in the current sprint','The number of hours logged by each team member','The count of defects closed since the last release','A','Forecasting uses observed throughput over several sprints against the size of the remaining backlog, which is what velocity and a burn-up chart express. Commitment, logged hours and defect closures do not measure delivery rate against remaining work.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Business Environment','hard','A project operating across two countries must meet conflicting data-residency requirements. The sponsor asks the project manager to choose the cheaper jurisdiction. What is the right response?','Comply with the sponsor and document the instruction in the issue log','Split the difference by storing half the data in each jurisdiction','Delay the project until one country changes its law','Present the compliance obligation as a constraint rather than an option, and bring alternatives that satisfy both jurisdictions with their cost','D','A legal obligation is a constraint on the solution space, so the project managers job is to present compliant alternatives and their cost rather than to trade compliance for price. Documenting an instruction to breach does not transfer the liability away from the organisation.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','People','exam','A stakeholder analysis places a stakeholder as high power and low interest. What engagement approach fits?','Manage closely with frequent detailed involvement in every decision','Keep satisfied, with concise reporting that does not overload but preserves support','Monitor with minimal effort, since interest is low','Ignore, because low interest implies no influence on the outcome','B','High power with low interest calls for keeping the stakeholder satisfied through targeted, low-volume communication that protects support without demanding attention. Treating them as low priority risks a late, powerful intervention.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Process','exam','A quality audit finds that the team is following its process but the process itself produces defects. What does this distinguish?','Quality control has failed while quality assurance succeeded','Quality assurance has failed, because the process itself is not fit to deliver the requirement','Both have failed equally','Neither has failed, because the team followed the documented process','B','Quality assurance concerns whether the process is capable and appropriate, while quality control concerns whether the output conforms. A followed but incapable process is an assurance failure by definition.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Business Environment','exam','What is the essential difference between a project and an operation?','Projects use external suppliers while operations use internal staff','Projects are always larger in budget than operations','Projects are always agile while operations are always predictive','A project is temporary and creates a unique result, while an operation is ongoing and repetitive','D','The defining characteristics are temporariness and uniqueness of the result, not size, sourcing or method. Confusing the two leads organisations to fund ongoing work as if it would end.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Process','hard','An activity has an optimistic estimate of 6 days, a most likely of 10 and a pessimistic of 26. What is the beta PERT expected duration and what does the spread tell the project manager?','12 days, and the wide spread signals significant uncertainty deserving reserve','14 days, and the estimate should simply replace the most likely value','10 days, because the most likely value dominates the calculation','16 days, and the pessimistic value should be used as the commitment','A','The beta PERT mean is optimistic plus four times most likely plus pessimistic divided by six, which gives seventy-two over six, that is twelve days. The long right tail relative to the mode is the signal that this activity carries risk needing contingency rather than a tighter point estimate.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','People','exam','A team member consistently delivers late but is the only person who knows a critical subsystem. What should the project manager do?','Escalate for immediate replacement of the team member','Accept the delays as an unavoidable consequence of the dependency','Address the performance directly while reducing the key-person risk through pairing, documentation and cross-training','Reassign the subsystem to someone else immediately','C','The situation is two problems at once: an individual performance issue and a single point of failure, and both need action. Removing the person before knowledge is transferred converts a delay risk into a delivery stoppage.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Business Environment','exam','Which situation most clearly requires the project manager to act on the PMI code of ethics rather than on stakeholder preference?','A stakeholder asks for an extra weekly meeting','A sponsor asks for the report format to be changed','A sponsor asks for a more optimistic completion date in the status report than the data support','A team member asks to work remotely two days a week','C','Reporting a date the evidence does not support is misrepresentation, which the responsibility and honesty values prohibit outright. The other requests are preferences within the project managers discretion.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Process','exam','What does a control chart of defect counts add to a project quality effort that an inspection log alone does not?','It separates common-cause variation from special-cause variation so the team knows when to intervene','It records who performed each inspection','It guarantees the defects will not recur','It replaces the need for a quality management plan','A','A control chart distinguishes ordinary process noise from signals that warrant investigation, preventing both over-reaction and under-reaction. An inspection log records events without that distinction.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','People','hard','A project manager inherits a team where several members openly distrust the previous leadership. What is the most effective first move?','Announce a new plan immediately to demonstrate decisive leadership','Replace the most vocal critics to reset the team culture','Report the trust problem to human resources and await guidance','Establish credibility by making and keeping small, visible commitments while listening to the teams account of what went wrong','D','Trust is rebuilt by demonstrated reliability on small things and by being heard, neither of which a new plan or a purge supplies. Listening also surfaces the structural causes that would otherwise repeat under the new leader.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Process','exam','A risk response transfers the financial consequence of a risk to a third party through insurance. What has and has not changed?','Both the probability and the impact have been eliminated','The probability is unchanged and the financial impact is borne by another party, while the operational consequence may remain','The risk is removed from the register entirely','The risk becomes an issue by definition','B','Transfer moves the financial consequence but does not reduce the likelihood of the event, and non-financial consequences such as delay usually remain with the project. The risk therefore stays in the register with a residual impact.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Business Environment','exam','An organisational process asset differs from an enterprise environmental factor in what way?','Organisational process assets are always external and environmental factors internal','Organisational process assets are internal templates, lessons and procedures the organisation controls, while environmental factors are conditions such as market or regulation','They differ only in which department maintains them','Environmental factors apply only to agile projects','B','Process assets are the reusable knowledge and procedures the organisation owns and can change, while environmental factors are conditions the project must work within. The distinction determines whether the project can influence the item or only respond to it.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','People','exam','What is the primary purpose of a team charter in an agile context?','To record the sprint backlog for the first iteration','To list the technical architecture decisions','To define the contractual obligations with the customer','To document the teams working agreements, values and decision-making norms','D','A team charter captures how the team will work together, including norms, meeting conventions and how decisions get made. Backlogs, architecture and contracts are separate artefacts.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Process','hard','A project manager finds that two activities each report zero total float but lie on different paths. What does this mean for schedule management?','There are two critical paths, so a delay on either will delay the project and both need monitoring','The schedule contains an error, since only one critical path can exist','One path must be the critical path and the other a near-critical path','Total float of zero indicates the activities are complete','A','A network can carry multiple paths with zero total float, and any of them will push the finish date if delayed. Treating only one as critical leaves the other unmonitored and is a common cause of surprise slippage.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Business Environment','exam','A programme differs from a large project chiefly because it does what?','Costs more than a defined organisational threshold','Runs for more than twelve months','Coordinates related components to obtain benefits not available from managing them separately','Uses only predictive life cycles','C','A programme exists to realise benefits that come from the interdependence of its components, which is a benefit management concern rather than a size concern. Cost, duration and life cycle are incidental.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Process','exam','What is the correct treatment of a management reserve in project cost management?','It sits inside the cost baseline and is spent by the project manager on identified risks','It is the same as contingency reserve under a different name','It sits outside the cost baseline, inside the project budget, and requires management approval to use','It is held by the seller under a fixed-price contract','C','Contingency reserve covers identified risks and sits inside the baseline under project manager control, while management reserve covers unknown unknowns, sits outside the baseline and is released by management. Confusing them lets unknown risk be spent silently.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','People','exam','A product owner keeps adding scope mid-sprint. What is the appropriate response?','Protect the sprint goal, refer the new items to the product backlog for the next sprint, and renegotiate only if the sprint goal itself is invalidated','Accept the additions, since the product owner sets priority','Extend the sprint until the additional work is complete','Escalate to the sponsor to have the product owner replaced','A','The sprint goal is what gives the team a stable timebox, and new work belongs to the backlog and the next planning event. The only legitimate mid-sprint renegotiation is when the goal itself no longer makes sense.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Process','hard','A schedule performance index of 1.10 accompanies a cost performance index of 0.78. What is the most plausible explanation?','The measurements are contradictory and one must be wrong','The project is genuinely performing well overall because the indices average above one','Earned value cannot produce indices in opposite directions','The project is ahead of schedule because extra resources or overtime were bought, which is exactly what depressed cost performance','D','Schedule and cost indices routinely move in opposite directions when time is bought with money, which is the crashing signature. Averaging the indices is meaningless because they measure different things against different baselines.'),
('PMP (Project Management Professional)','Business Environment','exam','Which artefact best demonstrates to an auditor that a project remained aligned to organisational strategy?','The daily standup notes','The traceability from strategic objectives through the business case to delivered requirements and measured benefits','The resource utilisation report','The list of change requests raised','B','Alignment is demonstrated by an unbroken chain from strategy to business case to requirements to measured benefit. Operational artefacts show activity rather than alignment.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'PMP (Project Management Professional)');

-- ============================================================================
-- CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development) — ATD Capability Model
-- ============================================================================
insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select * from (values
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Instructional Design','exam','A business partner requests a two-day workshop to fix low sales conversion. What should the talent development professional do first?','Design the workshop but reduce it to one day to save cost','Conduct a performance analysis to determine whether the gap is caused by skill, and if not, what else drives it','Deliver the workshop as requested, since the business partner knows the audience','Survey the sales team on their preferred training format','B','A requested solution is not evidence of a training need, and performance analysis separates skill gaps from process, incentive, tooling or management causes. Building a course for a non-training cause guarantees no change in conversion.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Evaluating Impact','exam','A programme shows very high satisfaction scores but no change in on-the-job behaviour. Which Kirkpatrick level failed and what does that imply?','Level one failed and the content must be made more engaging','Level four failed and the business case was wrong','Level two failed, meaning the learners did not acquire the knowledge','Level three failed, meaning learning was not transferred to the job, usually because the work environment does not support it','D','Behaviour is level three, and transfer failure normally comes from the environment: no manager reinforcement, no opportunity to apply, or competing priorities. High reaction scores at level one say nothing about transfer.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Learning Sciences','hard','A curriculum designer wants learners to retain procedural knowledge for at least six months. Which design choice is most strongly supported by learning science?','Distribute retrieval practice across spaced intervals rather than massing practice into one session','Increase the total contact hours in a single intensive session','Provide a comprehensive reference manual instead of practice','Match the delivery to each learners preferred learning style','A','Spacing and retrieval practice are among the most robust findings in the learning literature for durable retention, while massed practice produces fluency that decays quickly. Learning-style matching has repeatedly failed to show a retention benefit.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Training Delivery and Facilitation','exam','During a virtual session, participation collapses after the first twenty minutes. What is the most effective facilitator response?','Continue and rely on the recording for those who disengaged','Ask participants to keep cameras on for the rest of the session','Introduce a structured interaction such as a poll, breakout or applied task every few minutes','Shorten the remaining content by removing the practice activities','C','Virtual attention degrades quickly without deliberate interaction, so structured engagement at short intervals is the established remedy. Camera mandates and cutting practice address neither attention nor learning.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Performance Improvement','exam','An analysis finds that employees know how to complete a process correctly but consistently do not. Which intervention is indicated?','A refresher e-learning module on the process steps','A certification exam on process knowledge','Address consequences, feedback and process obstacles, because the gap is motivational or environmental rather than a knowledge gap','A longer onboarding programme for new hires','C','When capability exists but performance does not, the human performance technology model points to consequences, feedback, resources and process design. More instruction addresses a gap that the analysis has already excluded.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Talent Strategy and Management','exam','A talent strategy is being drafted for a rapidly growing function. What makes it a strategy rather than a plan of activities?','It links workforce capability requirements to business objectives and states the choices about build, buy and borrow','It lists every course to be delivered in the coming year','It specifies the learning management system to be procured','It sets the training budget by department','A','A talent strategy makes explicit choices about which capabilities matter for the business direction and how they will be sourced. Course catalogues, systems and budgets are the implementation of a strategy, not the strategy.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Data and Analytics','hard','A learning team reports that participants in a leadership programme were promoted at twice the rate of non-participants and claims the programme causes promotion. What is the flaw?','The comparison should have used a t-test rather than a ratio','The sample size is too small to support any inference','Promotion is a level two measure and cannot be used at level four','Selection bias, because high-potential employees are more likely to be nominated and to be promoted regardless of the programme','D','Participants were selected for potential, so the comparison confounds the programme with the selection criterion that also drives promotion. A defensible claim needs a matched comparison group or a pre-post design that controls for potential.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Change Management','exam','A new system rollout is meeting resistance from experienced staff. Which change approach fits best?','Mandate adoption with a compliance deadline and monitor usage','Involve the experienced staff early as design contributors and peer champions so their expertise shapes the rollout','Delay the rollout until resistance disappears naturally','Deliver additional technical training to everyone equally','B','Resistance from expert staff usually reflects a loss of mastery and of voice, and involving them converts that expertise into advocacy. Mandates raise compliance without commitment and uniform training ignores the actual concern.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Instructional Design','exam','Which learning objective is written correctly for an instructional design document?','Understand the escalation policy thoroughly','Given a customer complaint scenario, classify the escalation tier and route the case within two minutes with no misroutes','Be aware of the importance of escalation','Learn about escalation and its history','B','A usable objective states the condition, the observable behaviour and the criterion, so it can be assessed. Understand, be aware and learn about are not observable and cannot be measured.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Consulting and Business Partnering','exam','A senior leader asks for training and rejects the suggestion of a performance analysis. What is the most professional response?','Refuse the engagement until an analysis is approved','Deliver exactly what was asked and record the objection','Deliver a broader programme to cover every possible cause','Propose a rapid, low-cost analysis scoped to the leaders own deadline and framed around the business outcome he wants','D','Consulting credibility comes from meeting the client where the deadline and the outcome sit, while still protecting the diagnosis. Refusal ends the relationship and blind compliance guarantees a failed intervention.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Evaluating Impact','hard','A CFO asks for the return on investment of a safety programme. Incident costs fell by 480000 and the programme cost 120000. What is the ROI and what caveat must accompany it?','300 percent, with the caveat that the benefit must be isolated from other causes of the reduction','400 percent, with no caveat because the figures are audited','75 percent, because ROI is benefit divided by total of benefit and cost','360 percent, with the caveat that only direct costs count','A','ROI is net benefit divided by cost, so 480000 minus 120000 over 120000 gives three, or 300 percent. Any credible ROI must state how the benefit was isolated from other simultaneous influences, otherwise it is a correlation dressed as a return.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Technology Application','exam','A team is choosing between a learning experience platform and a traditional learning management system. What most cleanly distinguishes them?','A learning management system cannot host video content','A learning experience platform is always cheaper to operate','A learning management system administers assigned, tracked and compliance-bound learning, while a learning experience platform curates and recommends self-directed content','A learning experience platform is required for any accredited certification','C','The distinction is administration and compliance tracking against curation and discovery for self-directed learning. Cost and content type do not define the categories.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Learning Sciences','exam','Cognitive load theory suggests which design change when learners struggle with a complex procedure?','Add background theory before the procedure to give full context','Present all steps at once so learners see the whole picture','Segment the procedure, use worked examples first, and remove decorative material that adds extraneous load','Increase the pace so learners do not lose interest','C','Segmenting and worked examples reduce intrinsic and extraneous load while the schema forms, and decorative material competes for the same limited working memory. Adding theory or presenting everything at once increases the load that is already the problem.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Coaching','exam','In a coaching conversation the coachee describes a problem and asks the coach what to do. What is the most appropriate coaching response?','Ask questions that help the coachee generate and evaluate options before offering any perspective','Provide the recommendation, since the coach has more experience','Refer the coachee to a training course on the topic','End the session, because coaching is not advice giving','A','Coaching builds the capability to solve the next problem, which requires the coachee to generate and weigh options. Advice may follow with permission, but leading with it removes the developmental value.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Career and Leadership Development','hard','An organisation has strong technical talent but no successors for four critical leadership roles. What is the most effective structural response?','Promote the longest-serving technical expert into each role','Send all senior technical staff to a generic leadership course','Recruit externally for all four roles to guarantee capability','Build a succession plan tied to defined critical roles, with assessed readiness, development plans and stretch assignments','D','Succession planning identifies the specific roles, assesses readiness against their requirements and closes gaps through experience rather than courses alone. Generic training, blanket external hiring and tenure-based promotion all ignore role-specific readiness.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Knowledge Management','exam','A team of experts is retiring within eighteen months and their knowledge is undocumented. What is the most effective knowledge capture approach?','Ask each expert to write a comprehensive manual before leaving','Structure knowledge transfer through documented decision rules, shadowing and communities of practice with the successors','Record video interviews and store them in a shared drive','Purchase an external course on the same subject matter','B','Tacit expert knowledge transfers through structured interaction and codified decision rules rather than through documents alone. Manuals and video archives capture what the expert can articulate but not the judgement, and are rarely used afterwards.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Instructional Design','exam','What is the primary risk of designing assessment items before writing learning objectives?','Assessments will be too difficult for the audience','The assessment may measure content that the programme was never intended to change, breaking construct validity','The assessment will take longer to build','The assessment cannot be automated','B','Objectives define the construct the assessment is supposed to measure, so building items first invites misalignment between what is taught and what is tested. That misalignment invalidates any inference about learning.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Business Insight','exam','A talent development leader is asked to justify the function during a cost reduction. Which framing is strongest?','The number of training hours delivered per employee last year','The satisfaction scores achieved across all programmes','The size of the content library relative to competitors','The capability gaps closed against the two business objectives most at risk, with the cost of the alternative','D','Business insight means arguing in the language of business risk and alternatives rather than of activity volume. Hours, scores and library size measure effort, not contribution.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Evaluating Impact','exam','Which design most credibly isolates the effect of a training programme when a randomised trial is impossible?','A pre-post comparison with a matched non-participant comparison group','A post-programme survey asking participants how much the training helped','A comparison of this years results with last years overall results','A focus group of the highest performing participants','A','A matched comparison group absorbs the common trends that would otherwise be credited to the programme, which a simple pre-post or year-on-year comparison cannot. Self-report and selected high performers introduce further bias.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Compliance and Ethical Behavior','exam','A manager asks the talent development team for the individual assessment scores of his team from a development centre. What is the correct response?','Provide the scores, since the manager is accountable for the team','Provide the scores only for the lowest performers','Explain the confidentiality terms agreed with participants and offer aggregate or development-focused feedback within those terms','Refuse without explanation and escalate to human resources','C','Development assessments are normally gathered under a confidentiality agreement, and honouring it protects both the participants and the validity of future assessments. Aggregate or agreed development feedback meets the managers legitimate need without breaking the agreement.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Training Delivery and Facilitation','exam','A participant repeatedly challenges the facilitators credibility in front of the group. What is the best facilitation move?','Assert credentials firmly to re-establish authority','Ignore the participant entirely and continue the content','Acknowledge the point, invite the participant to contribute their experience to the group, and return to the objective','Ask the participant to leave the session','C','Acknowledging and redirecting converts a challenge into a contribution and keeps the group focused on the objective. Asserting authority escalates the conflict and ignoring it cedes the room.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Talent Strategy and Management','exam','Which measure best indicates whether an internal mobility strategy is working?','The proportion of open roles filled internally, together with the performance and retention of those internal hires','The number of internal job postings published','The number of employees who viewed the careers page','The average tenure of the workforce','A','A mobility strategy succeeds when internal candidates actually fill roles and then perform and stay, which needs both the fill rate and the downstream outcomes. Postings and page views measure activity, and tenure alone conflates mobility with stagnation.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Learning Sciences','hard','Which statement about transfer of learning is best supported by evidence?','Transfer is maximised by practising a single context until mastery','Far transfer occurs automatically once the underlying principle is understood','Transfer depends chiefly on the learners motivation at the time of instruction','Near transfer to similar contexts is easier to achieve than far transfer, and far transfer requires deliberate variation in practice contexts','D','Learners generalise poorly beyond the exact practice context, so varied practice contexts are what build the abstraction needed for far transfer. Single-context mastery produces fluency that does not travel.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Collaboration and Leadership','exam','A talent development team and the operations team disagree on release timing for mandatory training. What approach best serves the organisation?','Escalate immediately to the chief executive for a ruling','Surface the underlying constraints on both sides and design a schedule that meets the compliance deadline within operational capacity','Release on the talent development schedule, since compliance is non-negotiable','Postpone the training until operations has spare capacity','B','Collaboration works by exposing the real constraints, which usually reveals a schedule that satisfies both the deadline and the operational load. Unilateral release and indefinite postponement each sacrifice one partys legitimate constraint.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Instructional Design','exam','What distinguishes formative from summative assessment in programme design?','Formative assessment is graded and summative is not','Formative assessment happens during learning to guide adjustment, while summative assessment happens at the end to judge achievement','Formative assessment is only used in e-learning','Summative assessment must always be multiple choice','B','Formative assessment feeds back into the learning while it is still in progress, and summative assessment certifies the outcome. Grading, medium and item format are incidental to the distinction.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Data and Analytics','exam','A dashboard reports average completion time for an e-learning module as 42 minutes, but the distribution is bimodal at 12 and 70 minutes. What should the analyst conclude?','The average is a fair summary and the module length is appropriate','The module should be shortened to the average','The data are corrupted and should be discarded','The mean hides two distinct populations, so the module should be examined separately for each','D','A bimodal distribution means the mean describes no actual learner, and the two modes usually reflect different behaviours such as skipping versus engaging. Segmenting the analysis is the only way to act correctly.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Change Management','hard','A behaviour change initiative succeeded during the pilot but reverted three months after full rollout. Which explanation is most probable?','Reinforcement structures such as manager feedback, measurement and consequence were present in the pilot but not built into business as usual','The pilot participants were unrepresentative of the wider population','The training content was too difficult for the wider audience','The rollout was communicated too widely','A','Pilots often carry extra attention and support that quietly act as reinforcement, and removing them removes the sustaining mechanism. Sustainment requires the reinforcement to be designed into the operating routine, not into the project.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Professional Capability','exam','What is the primary purpose of a needs assessment report given to a business sponsor?','To present the recommended course outline and its duration','To document the training budget for the year','To state the performance gap, its causes, the recommended interventions and the expected business effect','To record the list of employees who will attend','C','The report exists to justify an intervention by connecting a measured gap to its causes and to a predicted business effect. Outlines, budgets and rosters follow once the sponsor accepts the diagnosis.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Training Delivery and Facilitation','exam','Which practice most improves the effectiveness of a role-play activity?','Assigning roles randomly without preparation to keep it authentic','Running the role play once with no debrief to save time','Providing clear scenario context, observable criteria and a structured debrief that links behaviour to the objective','Having the facilitator perform the role play while participants watch','C','The learning in a role play comes from the structured debrief against explicit criteria, not from the performance itself. Without criteria and debrief, participants rehearse habits rather than acquire skill.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Talent Strategy and Management','exam','A workforce planning exercise projects a shortfall of twelve specialists in eighteen months. Which response set is complete?','Evaluate build, buy, borrow and redesign options together against cost, lead time and risk','Train internally as the sole strategy','Reduce the demand by redesigning the work as the sole strategy','Recruit externally as the sole strategy','A','Workforce planning is a portfolio decision across developing internally, hiring, contracting and redesigning the work. Selecting one lever a priori ignores the lead time and cost differences that make the choice.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Emotional Intelligence and Decision Making','hard','A talent development leader notices she consistently rates candidates from her own former department more highly in development centres. What is the correct professional response?','Continue, because familiarity provides better contextual judgement','Stop using development centres altogether','Rate those candidates more harshly to compensate','Disclose the potential bias, remove herself from those ratings, and introduce structured criteria and a second independent rater','D','Recognising a systematic bias obliges disclosure and a structural control such as blind or independent rating against explicit criteria. Self-correcting by rating harshly substitutes one bias for another and remains unverifiable.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Evaluating Impact','exam','Which level of evaluation does a knowledge test administered immediately after a course address?','Level one, reaction','Level two, learning','Level three, behaviour','Level four, results','B','An immediate knowledge test measures acquisition of the taught content, which is level two. It says nothing about whether the knowledge is used on the job or whether business results changed.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Communication','exam','A talent development professional must present a programme proposal to an executive committee with ten minutes available. What structure is most effective?','A chronological account of the analysis performed','Lead with the recommendation and the business effect, then the evidence, then the ask, keeping detail in an appendix','A detailed walkthrough of the instructional design model applied','A live demonstration of the e-learning module','B','Executives decide on recommendation and consequence, so the ask must arrive early and the supporting evidence must be available but not narrated. Chronology and methodology consume the time before the decision is reached.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Performance Improvement','hard','A performance analysis identifies four causes of a service quality gap: unclear standards, missing job aids, no feedback loop and a skill gap in two of twelve staff. How should the intervention be sequenced?','Address the causes in the order the business sponsor prefers','Train all twelve staff first, because skill is the foundation of performance','Address only the skill gap, since that is the talent development mandate','Address standards, job aids and feedback first because they affect all twelve staff at low cost, then train the two with the skill gap','D','Environmental causes affect every performer and are usually the cheapest and fastest to fix, so they deliver most of the gain first. Training the two individuals is a targeted remedy that would otherwise be swamped by the systemic causes.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Technology Application','exam','What is the main risk of adopting an AI content generation tool for course authoring without a review process?','Plausible but inaccurate or non-compliant content can reach learners at scale and be treated as authoritative','It will not integrate with the learning management system','The tool will be too slow for practical use','Learners will find the content too easy','A','Generated content is fluent regardless of accuracy, and at scale an unreviewed error becomes an organisational standard. Subject-matter review before publication is the control that makes the productivity gain safe.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Instructional Design','exam','Which situation most justifies a job aid rather than a training programme?','The task is performed daily and speed matters more than accuracy','The task requires deep conceptual judgement under uncertainty','The task is performed rarely, is procedural, and errors are costly','The task is being introduced to the whole organisation at once','C','Job aids fit infrequent procedural tasks where recall would have decayed and the steps can be followed at the moment of need. Frequent tasks become automatic and judgement-heavy tasks cannot be reduced to a checklist.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Business Insight','exam','A learning function reports cost per learner as its headline efficiency metric. What is the principal weakness?','It is difficult to compute accurately','It cannot be compared across departments','It rewards cheap delivery regardless of whether capability or business outcomes improved','It is not recognised by finance functions','C','An efficiency metric with no effectiveness partner drives the function toward the cheapest delivery rather than the most valuable. Pairing it with a capability or outcome measure restores the trade-off.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Coaching','hard','A coachee sets a goal that the coach believes is unrealistic given the organisational context. What should the coach do?','Test the goal with the coachee against the context and evidence, so the coachee reassesses it themselves','Replace the goal with a realistic one drawn from the coaches experience','Report the concern to the coachees manager','Accept the goal without comment, because the coachee owns the agenda','A','Coaching preserves the coachees ownership while still bringing reality into the conversation through questioning and evidence. Silent acceptance abandons the coachs responsibility and substituting a goal abandons the coachees.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Career and Leadership Development','exam','What is the strongest argument for using stretch assignments in leadership development?','They are cheaper than external programmes','They allow the organisation to fill roles without recruitment','They are easier to schedule than classroom training','Most leadership capability develops through challenging real work with feedback and reflection, not through instruction alone','D','Development research consistently finds that difficult real assignments, supported by feedback and reflection, produce the largest capability gains. Cost and scheduling are secondary benefits rather than the rationale.'),
('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)','Cultural Awareness and Inclusion','exam','A global programme designed in one region shows much lower engagement in another. What is the most appropriate first response?','Translate the materials and redeploy unchanged','Investigate whether examples, cases and facilitation norms fit the local context, and localise accordingly','Reduce the programme length in the underperforming region','Replace the local facilitators','B','Low engagement across regions usually reflects cultural fit of examples, interaction norms and authority conventions rather than language alone. Localising the content and facilitation approach addresses the actual cause.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)');

-- ============================================================================
-- SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional) — behavioural competencies + HR expertise
-- ============================================================================
insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select * from (values
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Leadership and Navigation','exam','A chief executive announces a restructure to the board before consulting HR. What is the senior HR leaders most effective action?','Publicly correct the chief executive at the next board meeting','Immediately model the change plan, surface the workforce risks and legal exposure, and bring a costed implementation option to the chief executive','Decline to support the restructure until HR is properly consulted','Delegate the entire implementation to line managers','B','A senior HR leader adds value by converting a decision already taken into a risk-assessed, costed and lawful implementation rather than by contesting the process. Public correction and refusal both remove HR from the decision it needs to influence.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Business Acumen','exam','An HR leader wants to justify an investment in retention. Which quantification is most persuasive to a finance-led executive team?','The engagement survey score improvement expected','The industry benchmark turnover rate','The number of exit interviews conducted last year','The fully loaded replacement cost per departure multiplied by the avoidable turnover the intervention targets','D','Finance responds to an avoided cost tied to a defined population and a defensible unit cost. Benchmarks and survey scores describe a condition without translating it into money at risk.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Employee and Labor Relations','hard','A UAE-based employer wishes to terminate an employee on an unlimited contract for repeated unexcused absence. Under Federal Decree-Law 33 of 2021 what is the compliant path?','Follow the disciplinary procedure and the statutory notice period, unless the absence meets the specific statutory grounds for dismissal without notice','Terminate immediately in all cases, since unexcused absence is gross misconduct','Terminate only after the employee has completed one full year of service','Convert the contract to a limited term before terminating','A','The 2021 law requires notice and due process for ordinary termination and reserves dismissal without notice to an enumerated list of grounds, which includes absence for specified consecutive or aggregate days. Assuming every absence meets that threshold exposes the employer to an arbitrary dismissal claim.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Organizational Effectiveness and Development','exam','A merger brings together two workforces with incompatible performance cultures. What should the senior HR leader prioritise first?','Harmonise the pay structures across both organisations','Replace the leadership of the acquired organisation','Define the target operating model and the behaviours it requires, then align performance, reward and leadership signals to it','Run a joint engagement survey and publish the results','C','Culture integration follows from a defined operating model and the behaviours it needs, which then drive the reward and performance signals. Pay harmonisation and surveys without that definition produce activity without direction.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Critical Evaluation','exam','An engagement survey shows a ten point drop in one division. Before acting, what should the senior HR leader establish?','Whether the same drop occurred in the previous survey cycle only','Whether the division leader agrees with the result','Whether the drop exceeds the margin of error, whether response rates changed, and which specific items and subgroups moved','Whether the survey vendor can be replaced','C','A headline movement can be an artefact of sample composition or response rate, and only item-level and subgroup analysis identifies an actionable cause. Acting on the headline risks solving the wrong problem in the wrong population.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Total Rewards','exam','A compensation review finds that pay compression has placed several new hires above long-tenured peers doing identical work. What is the most defensible response?','Model the cost of correcting the affected internal population against the retention and legal risk of leaving it, and present options to the executive','Freeze new hire salaries until the market catches up','Disclose all salaries to the workforce to force transparency','Reduce the new hire salaries to restore the internal order','A','Pay compression is a quantified trade-off between correction cost and the retention and equity-claim risk of inaction, and the executive must choose with the numbers in front of them. Reducing existing pay is legally and contractually hazardous and freezing hiring pay simply defers the problem.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Consultation','hard','A business unit head asks HR to remove a low performer quickly, and the file contains no documented performance conversations. What is the correct senior HR response?','Support the removal, since business judgement on performance belongs to the manager','Refuse any action until twelve months of documentation exist','Advise the manager to reassign the employee quietly to another team','Explain the evidentiary and legal exposure, establish a short documented performance process, and identify any settlement route with its cost','D','Senior HR consultation names the risk, provides a lawful route and prices the alternatives, rather than either blocking or enabling. Quiet reassignment transfers the problem and an absolute documentation rule ignores that a settlement may be the rational option.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Workforce Management','exam','Headcount must fall by fifteen percent in six months. Which analysis must precede the selection of individuals?','A ranking of employees by manager preference','A definition of the future capability requirements and the roles that deliver them, with objective, documented selection criteria','A list of employees with the highest salaries','A voluntary redundancy offer to the entire workforce','B','Lawful and effective reductions start from the roles the future organisation needs and apply objective criteria, which is also what defends the decision later. Salary-based and preference-based selection creates discrimination exposure and removes capability at random.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Ethical Practice','exam','An executive asks HR to withhold from the board an investigation finding that implicates a senior leader. What must the HR leader do?','Withhold it, because the executive owns the board relationship','Ensure the finding reaches the governance body responsible for it, escalating through the audit or board channel if necessary','Release the finding to the whole workforce to force accountability','Delay the report until the leader has resigned','B','Ethical practice obliges HR to ensure that governance bodies receive the information they are accountable for, using the escalation channel when the normal one is blocked. Suppression makes HR complicit and broad disclosure breaches the confidentiality the investigation was conducted under.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','HR in the Global Context','exam','A company operating in the UAE and two other jurisdictions wants one global policy on working hours. What is the correct approach?','Apply the strictest jurisdictions rule everywhere for simplicity','Apply the headquarters jurisdiction rule everywhere','Apply the least restrictive rule to preserve flexibility','Set a global framework of principles with jurisdiction-specific annexes that meet each local statutory minimum','D','Statutory minimums differ and cannot be waived, so a global framework must carry local annexes that meet each one. Applying one jurisdictions rule everywhere is either unlawful in some locations or needlessly costly in others.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Critical Evaluation','hard','An analysis shows a strong negative correlation between training hours and turnover, and an executive proposes doubling training to halve turnover. What is the senior HR leaders objection?','The correlation may be driven by tenure or role, and reverse causation is plausible because employees who intend to leave stop attending training','The correlation coefficient is not statistically significant at this sample size','Turnover should be measured annually rather than monthly','Training hours is not a valid HR metric','A','Both variables track engagement and tenure, and departing employees disengage from training before they leave, which produces the correlation without the causal path assumed. A defensible claim needs a controlled comparison or at minimum stratification by tenure and role.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Structure of the HR Function','exam','An HR function is moving to a business partner model but partners are still processing transactions. What structural correction is needed?','Add more business partners to absorb the transactional load','Return to a generalist model','Establish a shared services capability and self-service so transactions leave the partner role, and hold partners to advisory outcomes','Outsource the business partner role entirely','C','The business partner model only works when the transactional layer is genuinely elsewhere and the partner is measured on advisory outcomes. Adding headcount to an unchanged structure scales the wrong work.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Talent Acquisition','exam','A senior HR leader observes that time to fill has improved but quality of hire has fallen. What is the most likely structural cause?','The compensation offered has become uncompetitive','The employer brand has weakened in the market','Recruiters are being measured only on speed, so selection rigour is being traded away','Candidates are applying to more employers simultaneously','C','A metric improvement accompanied by a quality decline is the classic signature of a single-metric incentive. Balancing the scorecard with quality of hire and early attrition restores the trade-off the recruiters face.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Leadership and Navigation','exam','HR proposes a change that two of five executives oppose. What is the most effective influence strategy?','Understand the specific interests behind each objection and adapt the proposal to meet them where it does not compromise the outcome','Proceed on the majority and let the opposition adjust','Escalate to the chief executive for a directive','Withdraw the proposal and resubmit unchanged next quarter','A','Influence at executive level works by addressing the underlying interest rather than by winning the vote, because implementation depends on the dissenters. A directive secures compliance without the ownership the change requires.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Risk Management','hard','An organisation plans to use an algorithmic screening tool for hiring. What is the senior HR leaders principal governance obligation?','Ensure the tool is faster and cheaper than the manual process it replaces','Ensure the vendor holds a recognised certification','Ensure the tool is trained only on the organisations own historical hiring data','Ensure the tool is validated for job-relatedness, tested for adverse impact across protected groups, explainable and subject to human review','D','Automated screening is a selection procedure and must meet the same job-relatedness and adverse impact standards as any other, with a human accountable for the decision. Training only on historical data is a known amplifier of past bias rather than a safeguard.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Employee Engagement and Retention','exam','Exit data show that regretted attrition concentrates in employees with eighteen to thirty months of service. What does this pattern most likely indicate?','A compensation problem across the whole organisation','A career path and development gap that appears once the initial learning curve is exhausted','A recruitment marketing problem','A problem with the onboarding programme in the first week','B','Departure after the learning curve flattens usually reflects the absence of a visible next step rather than pay or onboarding. Targeting career pathing at that tenure band addresses the actual inflection point.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Business Acumen','exam','A senior HR leader reviewing the operating plan sees that revenue growth of thirty percent is planned with flat headcount. What is the correct HR contribution?','Approve the plan, since flat headcount reduces HR cost','Model the productivity assumption implied, identify where capability or automation must close the gap, and flag the burnout and attrition risk','Request a proportional headcount increase','Defer to the finance function on workforce assumptions','B','HR contributes by making the implicit productivity assumption explicit and naming what must change for it to hold. Approving or simply requesting headcount both skip the analysis the executive team needs.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Diversity Equity and Inclusion','exam','Representation at entry level matches the labour market but falls sharply at the two most senior levels. Where should the intervention be focused?','On increasing entry level hiring further','On mandatory unconscious bias training for all staff','On external employer branding campaigns','On the promotion, succession and stretch-assignment processes where the attrition of representation occurs','D','A funnel that is healthy at entry and narrows higher up localises the problem in progression rather than in attraction. Interventions must target the promotion and sponsorship mechanisms where representation is actually lost.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Employee and Labor Relations','hard','A UAE employee resigns after four years of continuous service on an unlimited contract. What governs the end-of-service entitlement calculation?','Twenty-one days of basic wage for each of the first five years and thirty days for each subsequent year, based on the final basic wage','One month of total salary for every year of service including allowances','A flat statutory amount irrespective of length of service','No entitlement arises on resignation, only on employer termination','A','Federal Decree-Law 33 of 2021 sets end-of-service gratuity at twenty-one days basic wage per year for the first five years and thirty days per year thereafter, calculated on basic wage excluding allowances. Resignation after completing one year does not extinguish the entitlement under the current law.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Organizational Effectiveness and Development','exam','A senior leader asks HR to run a culture change programme consisting of workshops and a values launch. What is the strongest HR counsel?','Deliver as requested, since executive sponsorship guarantees success','Recommend a longer workshop series to reach every employee','Recommend that the visible systems, which are promotion, reward, resource allocation and what leaders tolerate, be changed first, with communication supporting them','Recommend replacing the values statement with a shorter one','C','Culture follows the behaviour that the organisation actually rewards and tolerates, so systems change is the lever and communication is the amplifier. Workshops and launches without system change produce cynicism.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Relationship Management','exam','A long-standing conflict between two department heads is damaging cross-functional delivery. What is the most effective senior HR intervention?','Recommend that one of them be reassigned','Deliver team-building training to both departments','Facilitate a structured conversation about the shared objectives and the specific behaviours each needs from the other, with follow-up accountability','Document the conflict and monitor it','C','Structured facilitation around shared objectives and concrete behavioural requests converts a personality framing into a workable agreement. Training and monitoring do not address the specific interdependence that is failing.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Technology Management','exam','An HR information system implementation is over budget and the executive asks whether to continue. What analysis should HR present?','The remaining cost to complete against the remaining benefit, plus the cost and risk of the alternatives including stopping','The amount already spent and the effort invested by the team','The vendor satisfaction scores from the project team','A comparison of the vendor against three competitors not evaluated originally','A','The forward decision turns on remaining cost against remaining benefit and the alternatives, since past spend is sunk. Presenting sunk cost invites the escalation-of-commitment error.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Total Rewards','hard','An organisation wants a variable pay plan for a support function whose output is hard to measure individually. What design is most defensible?','Individual commission based on estimated contribution to revenue','No variable pay at all, since measurement is imperfect','Discretionary manager awards with no stated criteria','A team or function-level plan on measurable shared outcomes, with an individual behavioural modifier against published criteria','D','Where individual output cannot be isolated, shared measurable outcomes plus a transparent behavioural modifier preserve line of sight without inventing a false individual metric. Fabricated individual metrics and unpublished discretion both damage perceived fairness.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Consultation','exam','A business leader presents HR with a solution and asks for implementation support. What should the senior HR professional do before agreeing?','Agree immediately to preserve the relationship','Clarify the outcome the leader wants and test whether the proposed solution is the one most likely to produce it','Refer the request to a junior HR business partner','Ask for the request in writing and process it as submitted','B','Consultation begins by separating the presenting solution from the intended outcome, because the two often diverge. Clarifying the outcome allows HR to add value without rejecting the leaders initiative.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Workforce Management','exam','Which workforce metric best supports a decision about whether to build or buy a scarce capability?','The current vacancy rate for the role','The internal time to develop the capability compared with the external time to hire, adjusted for cost and availability in the market','The number of applications received per posting','The average tenure of current holders of the role','B','Build or buy is a lead time and cost comparison under market availability, and both sides of that comparison must be quantified. Vacancy rates and application volumes describe the symptom rather than the choice.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Ethical Practice','exam','An employee raises a whistleblowing concern about the finance function to HR. What must HR ensure first?','That the concern is verified before any protection applies','That the concern is disclosed to the employees line manager','That the employee puts the concern in writing to the finance director','That the concern is routed to an independent channel, that confidentiality is preserved and that the employee is protected from retaliation','D','Protection from retaliation and an independent investigation route apply from the moment a concern is raised, not after it is proven. Routing it through the implicated function or the line manager destroys both independence and protection.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Business Acumen','hard','An HR leader must choose between two proposals with equal cost: one raises manager capability and one automates HR transactions. What decision basis is soundest?','Choose against the constraint that is currently limiting business performance, with the expected effect on that constraint quantified for each','Choose the capability programme because people matter more than systems','Choose whichever the chief executive prefers to preserve sponsorship','Choose the automation because its savings are easier to quantify','A','Equal-cost choices are decided by effect on the binding constraint, not by ease of measurement or general preference. Quantifying each option against that constraint makes the decision defensible to the executive.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Learning and Development','exam','A leadership programme is proposed for all managers at significant cost. What should the senior HR leader require before approval?','Confirmation that the vendor is well regarded in the market','A commitment that all managers will attend','A defined capability gap tied to business objectives, the population where the gap exists, and the measure that will show whether it closed','A guarantee of high satisfaction scores','C','Approval rests on evidence of a gap that matters to the business, in a specified population, with a defined success measure. Vendor reputation, attendance and satisfaction do not establish need or effect.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','HR in the Global Context','exam','An organisation is transferring employees between jurisdictions and asks HR to standardise the assignment package. What must the package accommodate?','Only the difference in base salary between locations','Only the tax treatment in the home jurisdiction','Tax equalisation, social security treatment, immigration status, local statutory entitlements and repatriation terms','Only the cost of housing at the destination','C','International assignment packages fail when any of the tax, social security, immigration or statutory entitlement elements is omitted, because each creates a legal or cost exposure. Repatriation terms prevent the assignment ending in an unmanaged separation.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Critical Evaluation','exam','HR reports that a wellbeing programme reduced absence by twelve percent. What single check most strengthens the claim?','Comparison against absence in a comparable non-participating population over the same period','A larger participant sample in the following year','A satisfaction survey of participants','Confirmation that the programme cost less than the absence saving','A','A concurrent comparison group absorbs seasonal and organisation-wide effects that would otherwise be attributed to the programme. Sample size, satisfaction and cost do not address attribution at all.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Employee and Labor Relations','hard','An investigation into a harassment complaint finds the allegation unsubstantiated but reveals a wider pattern of aggressive management behaviour. What is the correct outcome?','Close the case with no action, since the specific allegation was not substantiated','Uphold the complaint anyway to support the complainant','Refer the entire matter to external counsel and take no internal action','Close the specific allegation on its evidence while separately addressing the management behaviour identified, and inform the complainant of the process outcome','D','Findings must follow the evidence on the specific allegation, but material issues discovered during an investigation cannot be discarded because they fall outside the original complaint. Handling them as a separate management matter protects both fairness and the organisation.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Leadership and Navigation','exam','HR has limited credibility with the executive team. Which action rebuilds it fastest?','Increasing the volume and frequency of HR reporting','Selecting one business-critical problem, solving it measurably, and reporting the result in business terms','Rebranding the HR function and its service catalogue','Benchmarking HR headcount against peer organisations','B','Credibility is rebuilt by demonstrated contribution to a problem the executives already care about, expressed in their measures. Reporting volume, branding and benchmarks describe the function rather than its effect.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Corporate Social Responsibility','exam','An organisation publishes ambitious social commitments that its operating practices do not yet meet. What is the senior HR risk to flag?','That the commitments will be expensive to fund','That the gap between stated and actual practice damages employee trust and creates regulatory and reputational exposure','That competitors will copy the commitments','That the marketing function will control the narrative','B','The principal risk of unmatched commitments is the credibility gap, which erodes internal trust and invites external challenge. Cost and competitive imitation are secondary to the exposure created by the discrepancy.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Talent Acquisition','exam','A hiring manager insists on a degree requirement for a role where incumbents perform well without one. What should HR do?','Enforce the requirement, since the manager owns the role','Remove the requirement quietly without informing the manager','Refer the decision to the recruitment agency','Present the incumbent evidence, explain the adverse impact and talent pool cost of an unvalidated requirement, and agree job-related criteria instead','D','An unvalidated qualification narrows the pool and creates adverse impact exposure without predicting performance. Replacing it with job-related criteria, supported by the incumbent evidence, is both lawful and better selection.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Organizational Effectiveness and Development','hard','A matrix structure is producing chronic delay because decisions require agreement from both axes. What structural intervention is most likely to work?','Assign each decision type a single accountable decision owner with defined consultation, so agreement is not required for every decision','Add a coordination layer above both axes to arbitrate','Abolish the matrix and revert to a functional structure','Increase the frequency of cross-functional meetings','A','Matrix delay is a decision rights problem rather than a structure problem, and naming a single accountable owner per decision type removes the requirement for consensus. Extra layers and more meetings add cost to the same ambiguity.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Risk Management','exam','What is the primary purpose of a workforce risk register at executive level?','To record every employee relations case for audit','To track the HR budget against plan','To make workforce risks such as key-person dependency, capability gaps and compliance exposure visible with owners and mitigations','To document the performance ratings distribution','C','A workforce risk register exists to give the executive visibility of people risks with named owners and mitigations, in the same form as other enterprise risks. Case logs, budgets and ratings are operational records, not risk governance.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Employee Engagement and Retention','exam','Which practice most reliably converts engagement survey results into change?','Publishing the organisation-wide results with an executive commentary','Repeating the survey more frequently','Requiring each leader to select a small number of specific actions at team level, with progress reviewed as a normal business measure','Benchmarking the results against the industry','C','Engagement improves where local leaders own a small number of concrete actions that are tracked like any other commitment. Publication, frequency and benchmarking generate information without accountability.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Business Acumen','exam','An HR leader is asked which single workforce metric belongs on the executive dashboard. Which choice is most defensible?','The metric most tightly coupled to the current strategic constraint, such as critical-role vacancy duration during a growth phase','Training hours per employee, because it shows investment','Headcount, because it drives cost','Turnover, because it is the standard HR measure','A','A dashboard metric earns its place by informing the decision the executive currently faces, which changes with the strategic constraint. Standard metrics chosen by convention often measure a constraint that is not currently binding.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Ethical Practice','hard','HR discovers that a highly successful sales director has systematically misclassified expenses, though the amounts are small. The chief executive wants no action because of the revenue at stake. What must HR do?','Wait until the amounts become material before acting','Accept the executive decision, since revenue impact is a legitimate business consideration','Take informal action without documentation to avoid a confrontation','Apply the disciplinary standard consistently and escalate through governance if the executive blocks it, documenting the decision and its rationale','D','Selective enforcement destroys the standard for everyone and creates direct liability, so consistency must be preserved and the decision recorded at the level accountable for it. Deferring until the amounts grow ratifies the behaviour.'),
('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)','Structure of the HR Function','exam','An organisation of 400 employees is considering a full three-legged HR model with centres of excellence. What is the principal risk?','That the model is too modern for the workforce','That at this scale the specialisation creates handoffs and cost without the volume that makes centres of excellence efficient','That the model requires an external consultancy to implement','That business partners will become too influential','B','The three-legged model earns its overhead at scale, and below it the specialised roles fragment work that a generalist could complete end to end. Matching the operating model to the size of the organisation is the design decision.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)');

-- ============================================================================
-- SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional) — behavioural competencies + HR expertise, operational
-- ============================================================================
insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select * from (values
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Talent Acquisition','exam','A hiring manager wants to ask candidates about their family plans to assess availability for travel. What should the HR professional advise?','Allow the question if it is asked of every candidate equally','Replace it with a job-related question about ability to meet the travel requirement, asked of all candidates','Allow it only for senior roles','Ask the question during the reference check instead','B','Questions about family status invite discrimination claims and do not predict performance, whereas asking directly about the job requirement does. Asking every candidate an improper question does not cure the impropriety.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Employee and Labor Relations','exam','An employee complains informally about a colleagues behaviour and asks HR to keep it entirely secret. What is the correct response?','Agree to full secrecy to preserve trust','Refer the employee to their line manager and take no note','Open a formal investigation immediately without the employees knowledge','Explain the limits of confidentiality, that the concern will be handled discreetly but may require action, and record the conversation','D','HR cannot promise absolute confidentiality where a duty to act may arise, and saying so at the outset protects both parties. Recording the conversation preserves the evidence trail without escalating prematurely.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Total Rewards','hard','An employee in the UAE on a monthly basic wage of AED 12000 plus AED 3000 allowances completes three years and resigns. On what figure is the end-of-service gratuity calculated and for how many days per year?','On the basic wage of AED 12000, at twenty-one days per year of service for the first five years','On the total of AED 15000, at thirty days per year of service','On the basic wage of AED 12000, at thirty days per year of service','On the total of AED 15000, at twenty-one days per year of service','A','Federal Decree-Law 33 of 2021 bases the gratuity on the basic wage, excluding allowances, at twenty-one days for each of the first five years. Using the gross figure or the thirty-day rate overstates the entitlement materially.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Learning and Development','exam','A department requests the same compliance training for the fourth year running, with no change in outcomes. What should HR propose?','Deliver it again because compliance requires annual training','Reduce the length to save time','Check whether the recurring failures stem from process design or job aids rather than knowledge, and target the intervention accordingly','Outsource the delivery to a vendor','C','Repeating an intervention that has not changed outcomes indicates the cause is not a knowledge gap. Diagnosing whether the process or the point-of-work support is the cause is the only route to a different result.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Employee Engagement and Retention','exam','New hire turnover in the first ninety days is unusually high. Which investigation is most likely to find the cause?','A review of the compensation benchmark for all roles','A survey of employees with more than three years of service','Comparing the role as advertised and described at interview with the actual first-ninety-day experience, and reviewing manager onboarding practice','An analysis of the exit interviews of long-tenured leavers','C','Very early turnover usually reflects a mismatch between the expectations set during hiring and the reality of the job or the manager relationship. Long-tenure data and market benchmarks do not speak to that window.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Communication','exam','HR must announce a change to the medical benefit that will increase employee contributions. What communication approach is most effective?','Advance notice explaining the reason and the alternatives considered, with the specific effect per employee group and a channel for questions','An announcement made only through line managers with no written material','A single all-staff email on the effective date','A notice on the intranet without individual notification','A','Employees accept an unwelcome change far better when they receive advance notice, a rationale and a personalised effect, with somewhere to ask questions. Late or impersonal announcements convert a cost change into a trust problem.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Workforce Management','hard','A UAE employer requires an employee to work on a Friday which is a rest day under the contract. What does Federal Decree-Law 33 of 2021 require?','No compensation is required if the employee agrees in advance','The employee must be paid for the day at the normal rate only','The employee must be given two substitute rest days','The employee must be given a substitute rest day or the wage for that day plus an increase of not less than fifty percent of the basic wage','D','The law entitles an employee required to work on a rest day to either a compensatory rest day or the normal wage for that day plus at least fifty percent of the basic wage. Consent does not remove a statutory minimum entitlement.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Ethical Practice','exam','An HR professional is asked by a friend outside the organisation whether a named employee still works there and what their salary is. What is the correct response?','Confirm employment but not salary, since employment is public','Decline to disclose any employee information without authorisation and refer any formal verification request to the proper process','Provide both, since the friend will not disclose it','Provide the information but ask the friend to keep it confidential','B','Employment and pay data are confidential personal data regardless of the requesters relationship, and verification requests have a defined authorised channel. Partial disclosure is still disclosure.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Talent Acquisition','exam','A structured interview is being designed. Which element most improves its predictive validity?','Allowing each interviewer to ask their own questions to get a rounded view','Asking every candidate the same job-related, behaviourally anchored questions and scoring them against a defined rubric','Extending the interview length to ninety minutes','Having the most senior available manager conduct the interview alone','B','Structure, consistency and anchored scoring are what raise interview validity above unstructured conversation. Length and seniority do not compensate for inconsistent questions and unanchored judgement.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Performance Management','exam','A manager rates every member of the team as exceeding expectations, including two whose output has visibly declined. What is the most useful HR intervention?','Force a ranking distribution across the team','Instruct the manager to lower two ratings','Accept the ratings but note the concern in the file','Coach the manager on evidence-based rating and require specific documented examples supporting each rating','D','Rating inflation is corrected by requiring evidence for each rating, which changes the managers process rather than a single outcome. Forced distributions and imposed changes create resentment without improving the underlying judgement.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Employee and Labor Relations','hard','An employee is dismissed and claims the real reason was their complaint about safety two weeks earlier. What is the most important factual question for HR?','Whether the decision maker knew of the complaint and whether contemporaneous evidence shows the dismissal ground existed before it','Whether the complaint was ultimately upheld','Whether the employee had reached the end of their probation period','Whether the employee had been given a written warning at any time','A','A retaliation claim turns on decision-maker knowledge and on whether the stated ground pre-dated the protected act, which contemporaneous documentation establishes. Whether the complaint succeeded is irrelevant to whether raising it was protected.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Total Rewards','exam','What is the essential difference between a job evaluation and a market pricing exercise?','Job evaluation is only used for senior roles','Market pricing is required by law and job evaluation is optional','Job evaluation establishes internal relative worth of roles, while market pricing establishes external competitiveness against comparable roles','They produce identical results in a well-run organisation','C','Job evaluation answers the internal equity question and market pricing answers the external competitiveness question, and a pay structure needs both. They frequently disagree, which is exactly why both are performed.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','HR in the Global Context','exam','An employee will be seconded from the UAE to another country for eight months. Which HR item is most often overlooked and creates the largest exposure?','The choice of relocation vendor','The employees preferred housing type','The immigration and social security position, including whether the assignment triggers host-country tax residence','The frequency of home visits','C','Immigration status and tax or social security residence create legal and financial exposure for both the employee and the employer, and they turn on duration thresholds. Vendor and housing choices are logistical rather than legal.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Relationship Management','exam','A line manager routinely bypasses HR and makes his own employment decisions. What is the most effective HR response?','Understand what makes the HR route slow or unhelpful for him, fix that, and agree which decisions genuinely require HR involvement','Report the manager to his director for non-compliance','Restrict his system access to prevent unilateral action','Issue a written reminder of the policy to all managers','A','Bypassing usually signals that the official route is slower than the managers need, so removing the friction and clarifying the genuinely mandatory steps changes the behaviour. Reporting and access restrictions treat the symptom and damage the relationship.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Risk Management','hard','A workplace injury occurs and the employee asks HR not to report it because they fear consequences. What must HR do?','Honour the request, since the employee is the injured party','Report only if the injury requires more than first aid in the employees own judgement','Delay the report until the employee has recovered','Follow the mandatory reporting and recording obligations, while explaining the protection from retaliation the employee has','D','Injury reporting is a legal obligation that does not depend on the injured persons consent, and the correct response to fear of consequences is to explain the anti-retaliation protection. Suppressing or delaying a report exposes both the employee and the employer.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Employee Engagement and Retention','exam','Which use of stay interviews is most effective?','Conducting them only with employees who have resigned','Conducting them with high performers and high-risk roles before any intent to leave appears, and acting on the themes','Conducting them annually with the entire workforce as a survey','Conducting them only when a counter-offer is required','B','Stay interviews work because they surface retention drivers while the employee is still open to change, and their value depends on visible action on the themes. Once resignation occurs the conversation is an exit interview.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Learning and Development','exam','A new manager programme has high satisfaction but managers report they cannot apply it because their own directors work differently. What is the underlying issue?','The programme content is outdated','The learning environment is not reinforced by the level above, so the transfer conditions are absent','The programme is too short','The wrong managers were selected','B','Behaviour taught to one level and not modelled by the level above will not survive contact with the workplace. Engaging the directors in the same expectations is the condition for transfer.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Structure of the HR Function','exam','An HR coordinator spends most of the week answering the same twenty policy questions. What is the highest-value improvement?','Hire a second coordinator','Instruct employees to consult the policy manual themselves','Reduce the number of policies','Publish a searchable answer set for the recurring questions and route only exceptions to HR','D','Recurring identical questions are a self-service problem, and answering them at source frees the HR time for exception handling. Adding headcount scales the inefficiency and simply pointing at a manual has already failed.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Critical Evaluation','hard','An HR report states that turnover is 18 percent and the executive asks whether that is good. What is the most useful clarification before answering?','Whether the figure separates voluntary from involuntary, and regretted from non-regretted, and how it distributes across critical roles','Whether the figure was calculated monthly or annually only','Whether the industry benchmark is available','Whether the figure includes contractors','A','A single turnover number blends departures with entirely different meanings, and only the voluntary, regretted and critical-role breakdowns tell the executive whether to act. Benchmarks are useful only once the composition is known.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Performance Management','exam','What is the primary purpose of a performance improvement plan?','To create documentation supporting a dismissal already decided','To reduce the employees pay until performance improves','To give the employee a defined, measurable opportunity to reach the required standard with support and a clear timeframe','To transfer the employee to a different manager','C','A performance improvement plan is a genuine remediation instrument with measurable standards, support and a timeframe, and it must be capable of being passed. Used as pre-dismissal paperwork it is both unethical and legally fragile.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Talent Acquisition','exam','A role has attracted 300 applications and the manager wants HR to shortlist quickly. What is the most defensible screening method?','Screen on years of experience alone as a proxy for capability','Screen out any candidate with an employment gap','Screen against the documented essential criteria from the job description, applying them consistently and recording the reason for each rejection','Screen by education institution ranking','C','Consistent application of documented essential criteria is both the most valid screen and the record that defends the decision later. Proxies such as tenure, gaps and institution prestige add adverse impact without predictive value.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Technology Management','exam','HR wants to introduce an employee self-service portal. What is the most important adoption factor?','That the self-service route is genuinely faster and complete for the employee than asking HR directly','The visual design of the portal','That use of the portal is mandated by policy','That the portal is built by an external vendor','A','Self-service is adopted when it is the path of least resistance, and abandoned when it is incomplete and the employee must contact HR anyway. Mandates without that condition produce workarounds.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Diversity Equity and Inclusion','exam','An employee resource group asks for budget and executive sponsorship. What makes the request most likely to deliver organisational value?','Approving the budget with no defined objectives, to avoid constraining the group','Requiring the group to report to the marketing function','Restricting membership to the population the group represents','Linking the group to specific objectives such as retention or progression in a defined population, with a sponsor accountable for progress','D','Resource groups deliver measurable value when they carry defined objectives and an accountable executive sponsor. Unstructured funding produces activity that cannot be evaluated or defended at budget time.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Employee and Labor Relations','exam','Two employees give directly contradictory accounts in an investigation and there are no witnesses or documents. How should the investigator proceed?','Close the case as unresolvable','Weigh the credibility of each account against consistency, plausibility, contemporaneous conduct and any corroborating circumstance, and reach a finding on the balance of probabilities','Decide in favour of the more senior employee','Refer the matter to the police','B','Investigations reach findings on the balance of probabilities using credibility indicators, not on the presence of a witness. Closing as unresolvable or deferring to seniority abandons the duty to decide.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Consultation','exam','A manager asks HR for a policy exception for one employee. What should HR establish first?','Whether the manager has budget for the exception','Whether the exception has a legitimate business or legal basis, and what precedent granting it would create for comparable cases','Whether other employees will find out','Whether the employee has requested it in writing','B','Exceptions are governed by their justification and by the precedent they set for comparable circumstances, which is what keeps the policy defensible. Budget, secrecy and paperwork do not determine whether the exception is sound.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Total Rewards','exam','An employee asks why their colleague in the same job title earns more. What is the appropriate HR response?','Disclose the colleagues salary and its justification','Refuse to discuss pay in any form','Advise the employee that pay discussions are prohibited','Explain the pay structure, the factors that legitimately create differences such as experience and performance, and review the employees own position against the range','D','HR can and should explain the structure and legitimate differentiating factors and review the individual position, without disclosing another persons pay. Prohibiting pay discussion is both unhelpful and, in many jurisdictions, unlawful.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Workforce Management','exam','A department wants to convert several full-time roles to contractor engagements to reduce cost. What is the principal HR risk to raise?','That misclassification exposes the organisation to back-dated entitlements and penalties if the working relationship remains that of an employee','That contractors will be harder to schedule','That contractors will require different equipment','That the department will lose headcount reporting','A','Classification depends on the substance of the working relationship, not on the label in the contract, and misclassification carries retrospective liability. Scheduling and equipment are operational details by comparison.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Learning and Development','hard','An HR professional must evaluate a proposed e-learning vendor. Which evidence is strongest that the product will change behaviour?','A large library of courses and a modern interface','A high average learner satisfaction rating','Case evidence of behaviour or performance change in a comparable organisation, with the measure and the comparison stated','A competitive price per licence','C','Only evidence of behaviour or performance change in a comparable setting, with a stated measure and comparison, speaks to effect. Library size, satisfaction and price describe the product rather than its outcome.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Ethical Practice','exam','An HR professional discovers a payroll error that overpaid an employee for six months. The employee has spent the money. What is the correct approach?','Recover the full amount immediately from the next salary payment','Write off the amount without telling the employee','Inform the employee, explain the error, and agree a lawful repayment arrangement consistent with the employment terms and deduction rules','Report the employee for failing to notify the employer','C','Overpayment recovery must respect the statutory and contractual limits on deductions and requires the employee to be informed and consulted. Immediate full recovery may be unlawful and silent write-off conceals a control failure.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Employee Engagement and Retention','exam','A team reports low psychological safety in a survey. Which manager behaviour most directly improves it?','Responding to mistakes and dissent with curiosity and problem-solving rather than blame, and admitting their own errors','Reducing the frequency of team meetings','Increasing individual performance targets','Introducing anonymous reporting only','A','Psychological safety is built by the observable response to error and disagreement, which the manager demonstrates repeatedly. Anonymous channels are a fallback that does not change the everyday signal.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Performance Management','hard','An organisation replaces annual ratings with continuous feedback but keeps a forced annual pay differentiation. What problem should HR anticipate?','Managers will hold fewer feedback conversations because there is no rating','The system will be too expensive to administer','Employees will demand more frequent surveys','Pay decisions will still require an implicit ranking, so an undocumented and unexplainable rating will operate in the shadows','D','Differentiated pay always requires a comparative judgement, so removing the visible rating without removing the differentiation drives the judgement underground. That makes pay decisions harder to explain and to defend.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Communication','exam','HR must deliver difficult feedback to a long-serving employee. Which approach is most effective?','Open with extensive praise to soften the message','Describe the specific behaviour and its observed effect, check understanding, and agree the change required with a follow-up point','Deliver the message in writing only, to create a record','Have the employees peer deliver the message','B','Specific behaviour, observed effect and an agreed change with follow-up is what makes feedback actionable and fair. Padding obscures the message, and written-only or peer-delivered feedback removes the dialogue that produces understanding.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Talent Acquisition','exam','What is the most reliable measure of the quality of a recruitment process?','The number of applications received per vacancy','The performance and retention of hires at twelve months, compared across sourcing channels','The speed of the offer decision','The cost per hire','B','Quality of hire is observed after the hire, and comparing it across channels tells the organisation where its good hires actually come from. Volume, speed and cost are efficiency measures that can improve while quality falls.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','HR in the Global Context','exam','A policy written for one country is being rolled out to a second. What must HR verify first?','That the policy has been translated accurately','That the local managers approve the tone','That the policy has the same page count','That the policy meets or exceeds the statutory minimums of the second jurisdiction and does not conflict with its mandatory provisions','D','A policy cannot contract below a statutory minimum, so legal compatibility precedes every other consideration. Translation matters, but a faithfully translated unlawful policy is still unlawful.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Risk Management','hard','An employee requests an adjustment to their working arrangement for a medical condition. The manager says the role cannot be performed any other way. What must HR do before accepting that?','Conduct an individualised assessment of the essential functions of the role against the specific adjustment requested, exploring alternatives with both parties','Require the employee to obtain a specialist medical report before any discussion','Accept the managers operational judgement, since he owns the role','Offer the employee a different role immediately','A','The obligation is to assess the specific request against the essential functions of the role, individually and in dialogue with the employee, before concluding that no adjustment is possible. A blanket managerial assertion is not an assessment and leaving the process there creates direct exposure.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Structure of the HR Function','exam','HR is asked to justify its headcount. Which framing is strongest?','The HR to employee ratio compared with an industry benchmark','The number of years each HR team member has served','The services delivered, their volume and cycle time, and what would have to stop or be outsourced at a lower headcount','The size of the HR budget relative to last year','C','A defensible justification states what the function delivers and what would be lost at a lower level, which is a service and capacity argument. Ratios and tenure describe the function without connecting it to output.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Employee and Labor Relations','exam','What is the primary purpose of documenting an informal conversation about a minor performance concern?','To begin the disciplinary process formally','To justify withholding a pay increase','To create a contemporaneous record of what was raised and agreed, so later steps rest on an accurate history','To share the concern with the employees colleagues','C','Contemporaneous notes preserve an accurate account of what was said and agreed, which is what makes any later formal step fair and defensible. Documentation is not itself a disciplinary step nor a basis for pay action on its own.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Critical Evaluation','exam','HR presents absence data showing one team at double the organisational rate. What should be checked before concluding the team has an absence problem?','Whether the team has a smaller headcount so a single long-term case distorts the percentage, and whether the absence type differs','Whether the team leader is popular with the team','Whether the team met its targets last quarter','Whether the team works in the same building as HR','A','Small denominators and a single long-term case routinely produce dramatic percentage differences that have no management meaning. Distinguishing short-term frequent absence from a single long-term case determines whether any intervention is appropriate.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Total Rewards','hard','An employer in the UAE wants to pay part of the monthly salary in cash outside the Wage Protection System to reduce administration. What should HR advise?','That it is acceptable provided the employee consents in writing','That it is acceptable for allowances but not for basic wage','That it is acceptable if the total paid matches the contract','That the full contracted wage must be transferred through the Wage Protection System, and partial payment outside it exposes the establishment to sanctions and blocked work permits','D','The Wage Protection System requires the contracted wage to be transferred through approved channels so that payment can be verified, and shortfalls trigger establishment-level sanctions including suspension of new work permits. Employee consent cannot waive a regulatory requirement placed on the employer.'),
('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)','Relationship Management','exam','An HR professional is caught between a manager who wants speed and an employee who wants a full process. How should the situation be framed?','As a choice between the two parties, to be resolved by seniority','As a shared interest in a decision that is both timely and defensible, with the minimum process that achieves both stated explicitly','As a matter for legal counsel from the outset','As a policy question with no room for judgement','B','Framing the tension as a shared requirement for a decision that will hold up, and naming the minimum process that achieves it, gives both parties a route they can accept. Deciding by seniority or deferring entirely to counsel abandons the HR judgement the situation calls for.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)');

-- ============================================================================
-- Proof: raise unless each of the five banks has >= 40 questions on production.
-- ============================================================================
do $$
declare
  r record;
  bad text := '';
begin
  for r in
    select c.certification, coalesce(q.n, 0) as n
    from (values
      ('CSSBB (Six Sigma Black Belt)'),
      ('PMP (Project Management Professional)'),
      ('CPTD (Certified Professional in Talent Development)'),
      ('SHRM-SCP (SHRM Senior Certified Professional)'),
      ('SHRM-CP (SHRM Certified Professional)')
    ) as c(certification)
    left join (
      select certification, count(*)::int as n
      from public.certification_exam_question
      group by certification
    ) q on q.certification = c.certification
  loop
    raise notice 'bank % has % questions', r.certification, r.n;
    if r.n < 40 then
      bad := bad || r.certification || '=' || r.n || '; ';
    end if;
  end loop;
  if bad <> '' then
    raise exception 'certification bank under 40 questions: %', bad;
  end if;
end $$;
